<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lorenzo de' Medici</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1449">1449</a>–<a href="/years/1492">1492</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Lorenzo the Magnificent · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-medici">House of Medici</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lorenzo de' Medici, called the Magnificent, was the effective ruler of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> from 1469 until his death. He survived the Pazzi conspiracy of 1478, in which his brother Giuliano was killed, and worked to keep peace among the Italian states through diplomacy. A poet and a patron of artists such as Botticelli and the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a>, he came to stand for the Florentine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lorenzo-de-medici.jpg" alt="Lorenzo de' Medici" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Workshop of Bronzino · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lorenzo_de_Medici.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lorenzo di Piero de' Medici was born on 1 January 1449. His grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-de-medici">Cosimo de' Medici</a> was the first of the family to lead the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> and run the Medici Bank at the same time, and his father Piero was a patron and collector of art. His mother, Lucrezia Tornabuoni, wrote sonnets and was a friend of the poets and philosophers of the Medici Academy. Lorenzo was tutored by the diplomat Gentile de' Becchi and the humanist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marsilio-ficino">Marsilio Ficino</a>, and was trained in Greek by John Argyropoulos.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Lorenzo took over the direction of the state on the death of his father in 1469, at the age of 20. Like his grandfather and father, he ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> indirectly, through surrogates in the city councils, by means of payoffs and strategic marriages. On 26 April 1478 a group led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girolamo-riario">Girolamo Riario</a>, Francesco de' Pazzi and Francesco Salviati, archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>, attacked Lorenzo and his brother Giuliano in the Cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence-cathedral">Santa Maria del Fiore</a>. Giuliano was stabbed to death, while Lorenzo escaped with a minor neck wound. The populace put down the conspiracy brutally, lynching the archbishop and members of the Pazzi family.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a>, whose blessing Salviati had, excommunicated Lorenzo and the Florentine government and placed the city under interdict. He then allied with King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-naples">Ferdinand I of Naples</a>, whose son Alfonso invaded the republic. The war dragged on until Lorenzo travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> himself and was for several months the king's prisoner; diplomacy ended the crisis. Lorenzo later followed a policy of keeping peace between the northern Italian states and keeping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p>He helped artists including Botticelli, Verrocchio, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci">Leonardo da Vinci</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a> to secure commissions, and Michelangelo lived for three years with his family. He wrote poetry in Tuscan and expanded the Medici Library begun by Cosimo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Several branches of the Medici Bank collapsed through bad loans during Lorenzo's lifetime. He died on the night of 8 April 1492 at the family villa of Careggi. His eldest son, Piero, known as the Unfortunate, squandered the inheritance and brought down Medici rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> in 1494. His second son Giovanni, who became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x">Pope Leo X</a>, retook the city in 1512 with the help of a Spanish army.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lorenzo de' Medici” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His grandson <b>Lorenzo de’ Medici</b> survived the Pazzi conspiracy of 1478, in which his brother Giuliano was killed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Later in the 15th century Angelo Poliziano, tutor to the sons of <b>Lorenzo de' Medici</b>, showed how to judge manuscripts by their age and descent.</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · People</a></li><li><blockquote>The book was first intended for Giuliano di <b>Lorenzo de' Medici</b>, who died in 1516, and it came to be dedicated to Lorenzo di Piero de' Medici, grandson of Lorenzo the Magnificent, whose uncle Giovanni became Pope Leo X in 1513.</blockquote><a href="/work/the-prince">The Prince · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The son of <b>Lorenzo the Magnificent</b> was announced as pope at 37.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-11">11 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Federico da Montefeltro, hired by Florence under <b>Lorenzo de' Medici</b>, besieged and sacked the city, which was then fully absorbed into the Republic of Florence.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-volterra">Commune of Volterra</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Careggi, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1476" aria-label="Florence on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Lorenzo de' Medici"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5691 3800h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5691 3800h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1476">What was here in 1476? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/piero-di-lorenzo-de-medici"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/piero-di-lorenzo-de-medici.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Piero di Lorenzo de' Medici</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; 1472–1503</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pope-leo-x.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Pope Leo X</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; 1475–1521</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Fair is youth and void of sorrow; But it hourly flies away.— Youths and maids, enjoy to-day; Naught ye know about to-morrow.&#8221;</p><footer>Song of Bacchus and Ariadne, a carnival song for Florence &#183; tr. John Addington Symonds, 1881 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/35792/pg35792-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Midas treads a wearier measure: All he touches turns to gold: If there be no taste of pleasure, What's the use of wealth untold?&#8221;</p><footer>Song of Bacchus and Ariadne, a carnival song for Florence &#183; tr. John Addington Symonds, 1881 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/35792/pg35792-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Ladies and gay lovers young! Long live Bacchus, live Desire! Dance and play, let songs be sung; Let sweet Love your bosoms fire; In the future come what may!&#8221;</p><footer>Song of Bacchus and Ariadne, a carnival song for Florence &#183; tr. John Addington Symonds, 1881 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/35792/pg35792-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
